"<meeting id=\"3989eee1-0033-4a72-b0f7-a37ec3b60834\" title=\"Ancient civilizations insights\" date=\"Aug 24, 2026 2:27 PM GMT+1\">\n  <known_participants>\n  Kris Brown (note creator) from Humans Not Robots &lt;kris@humansnotrobots.com&gt;\n  </known_participants>\n  \n  <summary>\n### Ancient Civilizations and Monuments\n\n-   Fascination with a time-lapse visualization of how civilizations emerged: Mesopotamia, Göbekli Tepe, Stonehenge, Machu Picchu, Aztec temples, pyramids\n-   Seven Wonders as a connective thread across cultures and eras\n-   Colossus of Rhodes as one example: one of the Seven Wonders, the giant warrior guarding the gates\n-   Debate on whether ancient monument-building reflects shared human ingenuity or something more exotic (extraterrestrial influence floated)\n\n### Ice Ages, Geology, and Lost Worlds\n\n-   Sea levels much lower during ice sheet periods\n-   Mediterranean likely filled catastrophically as ice melted: salt flats, evaporated lakes, then refilling via Gibraltar\n    -   Most plausible location for Atlantis, per the discussion\n-   Magnetic polarity shifts recorded in geological deposits: happened multiple times, potentially catastrophic in effect\n-   Sediment records give a lot of data, but the *why* behind ice ages remains unclear\n\n### Viking Reach and Revisionism\n\n-   Vikings traveled far beyond Scandinavia: Iceland, Canada, Gibraltar, Cyprus, Constantinople, Mediterranean\n    -   Got stranded on Iceland due to lack of trees for ship repair\n-   Reputation as purely violent raiders challenged\n    -   Early raids (e.g. Lindisfarne) likely rogue actors, not representative\n    -   When larger groups settled, they were largely civilized\n    -   Saxons, by contrast, massacred Viking settlers under the Danelaw\n-   Great Heathen Army (~AD 864): traveled the Humber, Ouse to York, Trent to Derby, using river systems extensively\n\n### Vikings, Normans, and 1066\n\n-   Dukes of Normandy were Viking descendants, given the region by the Gauls to stop further raids\n-   1066 reframed: not Saxon vs. Norman, but Viking vs. Viking\n    -   Harold Hardrada: Viking descent\n    -   Normans (William I): Viking descent via Rollo of Normandy\n    -   Rollo likely the same figure as “Vola,” great-great-great-grandfather of William the First, matching the Ragnar Lothbrok saga timeline\n-   Stamford Bridge and Hastings both within 200 years of peak Viking movement: still living history\n\n### Mythology Grounded in History\n\n-   Greek and Norse mythology increasingly traceable to real events and places\n    -   Troy and the Trojan War: real archaeological basis\n    -   Mount Olympus on Cyprus: possible real-world anchor for Greek gods (Aphrodite, etc.)\n-   Ragnar Lothbrok saga: names descendants including Ivar the Boneless, all part of the Great Heathen Army\n-   Pattern across cultures: mythology often encodes real battles, migrations, and geography\n</summary>\n</meeting>"
